WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.890
As Lt. Commander Data on Star Trek: The Next
Generation, Brent Spiner saved the Enterprise

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:10.420
on more than one occasion, and allowed the
show to explore ethical questions about just

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.139
what it means to be an android in a world
of humans.

00:00:13.139 --> 00:00:17.760
Despite being the least human of the cast,
Data quickly became a fan-favorite character,

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.340
all the way through the series' 1994 finale
and four subsequent films… but what happened

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.930
to Spiner after Star Trek ended?

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:27.570
Independence Day

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:32.529
In 1996, Spiner made an appearance in the
smash science fiction hit Independence Day,

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:37.020
though many fans may not have recognized him
after seeing him as the stoic Data for so

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.020
long.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:42.900
You can spot Spiner in the part of Dr. Brackish
Okun, the unkempt and enthusiastic director

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.700
of Area 51 who joins a surgical team during
an autopsy on one of the aliens.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:52.260
Although Okun appears to meet a messy end,
Spiner returned to reprise his role in the

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.430
2016 sequel.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:56.670
Introducing Dorothy Dandridge

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:02.440
In 1999, Spiner landed a co-starring role
alongside Halle Berry in the HBO production

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:08.070
Introducing Dorothy Dandridge, a bio-pic focusing
on the life of a singer, dancer, and actress

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:12.650
who battled against pervasive racial discrimination
during her incredible career.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:16.470
Spiner appears as Earl Mills, Dandridge's
friend and manager.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:20.470
Throughout the rise and fall of her too-brief
career, Mills stood by her side until the

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:23.790
end — the day he found her body after she
passed away at age 42.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:26.890
Return to Star Trek

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:32.229
Even though Data's storyline was wrapped up
during the course of 2002's Star Trek: Nemesis,

00:01:32.229 --> 00:01:34.920
the franchise wasn't completely done with
Spiner.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.920
While the door was left open for him to potentially
return as an identical android, Spiner felt

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.820
he'd gotten too old to play the ageless robot.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:49.280
But in 2004, he appeared on Star Trek: Enterprise
as Arik Soong, a criminal geneticist and ancestor

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.690
of Data's creator, Dr. Noonien Soong… who
was also played by Spiner.

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:58.110
By the end of Soong's arc in the prequel series,
the foundations are laid for the creation

00:01:58.110 --> 00:01:59.650
of Data.

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:01.240
Spiner on film

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.900
Although many of his film roles have been
relatively minor, Spiner has had some scene-stealing

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:11.260
moments, including his appearance in the 1996
John Travolta fantasy drama Phenomenon, as

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:12.779
an FBI doctor.

00:02:12.779 --> 00:02:18.290
In 2002, Spiner appeared as bad guy Devlin
Bowman in the notoriously awful Dana Carvey

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:20.340
comedy The Master of Disguise.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:25.099
The entire movie was a vehicle to showcase
Carvey's impressive mimicry skills, but it

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:26.099
didn't work.

00:02:26.099 --> 00:02:30.629
The Master of Disguise was a commercial and
critical flop, and playing a ridiculously

00:02:30.629 --> 00:02:33.280
flatulent villain didn't do Spiner any favors.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:34.580
"Is he dead?"

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:42.090
Spiner also appeared in the Jack Lemmon and
Walter Matthau comedy Out to Sea, and made

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:46.500
a brief appearance in the 2004 Howard Hughes
bio-pic The Aviator.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:52.020
In 2006, Spiner returned to the big screen
for another comedy, the teen-focused Material

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:57.680
Girls, starring Hilary and Haylie Duff, playing
the nefarious co-founder of a cosmetics company.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.879
Although it fared slightly better with critics
than The Master of Disguise, that also isn't

00:03:01.879 --> 00:03:02.909
saying much.

00:03:02.909 --> 00:03:07.169
The film was nominated for multiple Razzies
and was one of the worst-reviewed films of

00:03:07.169 --> 00:03:08.900
the 2000s.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.080
"Oh s---."

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:12.879
Threshold

00:03:12.879 --> 00:03:19.309
In 2005, Spiner starred along with Peter Dinklage
and Carla Gugino in the CBS sci-fi series

00:03:19.309 --> 00:03:20.309
Threshold.

00:03:20.309 --> 00:03:24.530
The trio formed the core of a secret U.S.
governmental think tank tasked with humanity's

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:31.260
first extraterrestrial contact, with Spiner
playing Dr. Nigel Fenway, a NASA microbiologist.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:35.529
The top-secret "Red Team" is in charge of
researching and containing an incident on

00:03:35.529 --> 00:03:40.499
an American naval vessel where several crew
members die horribly after encountering a

00:03:40.499 --> 00:03:41.499
UFO.

00:03:41.499 --> 00:03:45.609
Although it was canceled after only one season,
Threshold is a must-watch for Brent Spiner

00:03:45.609 --> 00:03:46.749
fans.

00:03:46.749 --> 00:03:48.150
Voice acting

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:52.499
Because Spiner has such a rich theater background,
it probably shouldn't be surprising that he's

00:03:52.499 --> 00:03:55.049
done plenty of voiceover work over the years.

00:03:55.049 --> 00:04:00.629
In the 1999 movie South Park: Bigger, Longer
&amp; Uncut, he provides the voice of talk-show

00:04:00.629 --> 00:04:02.069
host Conan O'Brien.

00:04:02.069 --> 00:04:06.889
He's also taken voice roles on several TV
shows, including the role of the Joker on

00:04:06.889 --> 00:04:09.069
Young Justice.

00:04:09.069 --> 00:04:15.909
"Ladies and gentlemen, we interrupt your regularly-scheduled
mayhem to bring you this."

00:04:15.909 --> 00:04:21.200
His credits also include Purple Man in The
Avengers: Earth's Mightiest Heroes, The Simpsons,

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:22.850
Star Wars Rebels, and Family Guy.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:23.870
"I want some McNuggets!"

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:25.880
"We'll get to you, Brent."

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:30.350
If you're a fan of the video game Elite Dangerous,
now you can get Spiner's voice in your spaceship;

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:34.160
he provided a voice pack for Vega in 2016.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:36.350
Fresh Hell

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:40.390
In 2011, Spiner co-created and starred in
his own web series, Fresh Hell.

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:44.610
The show depicts a fictionalized version of
Spiner trying to rebuild his career after

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:45.620
losing everything.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:50.230
Spiner's unexpected comedic talents shine,
and the episodes are short and funny.

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:53.430
Fans who haven't seen it yet should definitely
give it a watch.

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:58.550
Spiner has also made plenty of guest appearances
as himself on TV, including the Friends spinoff

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:03.310
Joey, and The Big Bang Theory, where he reunites
with his Next Generation costar Wil Wheaton

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:04.790
at a birthday party.

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:09.690
Spiner has also appeared as himself on The
Guild, as well as many other shows.

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:11.360
Warehouse 13

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:15.990
Spiner made his return to science fiction
in several episodes of the SyFy series Warehouse

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:18.120
13 during the show's fourth season.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:23.600
Over a six-episode arc, Spiner appears as
Brother Adrian, a Vatican agent and member

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:25.320
of the "Brotherhood of the Black Diamond."

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:28.140
Upon returning to science fiction, Spiner
told SyFy,

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:31.230
"I'm not like really zeroed in focused on
sci-fi.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:32.930
But if it's good sci-fi, I like it.

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:34.020
I like westerns too.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:35.070
I like comedy.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:39.140
I was much more of a Lucy kind of guy than
I was a Star Trek kind of guy.

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:42.120
But fate has taken me in this direction."

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:43.120
Outcast

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:48.860
Since 2016, Spiner's biggest project has been
the Cinemax horror series Outcast, in which

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:53.480
he plays Sidney, a mysterious preacher involved
in a tale of demonic possession in a small

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:54.950
West Virginia town.

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:59.790
For fans who loved Spiner's work as Data,
seeing him in such a dark role might be disturbing

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:04.160
at first, but it showcases a menacing side
of his acting — one we haven't really seen

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:07.480
since Data's brother Lore was deactivated
for good.

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:10.160
And playing a gassy villain doesn't count.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:11.280
"Hahaha."

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:14.940
Thanks for watching!

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.980
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:20.430
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

